A dangling pointer refers to an object whose lifetime has ended or whose storage is invalid. Dereferencing it is undefined behavior.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int* bad() {
    int x = 5;
    return &x;      // x dies when the function returns, so the pointer dangles
}

int good() {
    int x = 5;
    return x;       // return by value instead
}

int main() {
    // int* p = bad();   // *p would be undefined behavior
    std::cout << good() << "\n";
}
```

Fix: return by value, or use an ownership type like `std::unique_ptr` when you need heap memory. Compile with `-Wall` and `-fsanitize=address` to catch these bugs.

Breadth-first search visits graph vertices level by level using a queue. With an adjacency list it takes O(V + E) time and O(V) auxiliary space.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <queue>
#include <vector>

void bfs(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int start) {
    std::vector<bool> visited(adj.size(), false);
    std::queue<int> q;
    visited[start] = true;      // mark when enqueuing
    q.push(start);
    while (!q.empty()) {
        int u = q.front();
        q.pop();
        std::cout << u << " ";
        for (int v : adj[u]) {
            if (!visited[v]) {
                visited[v] = true;
                q.push(v);
            }
        }
    }
    std::cout << "\n";
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj = {{1, 2}, {3}, {3}, {}};
    bfs(adj, 0);   // 0 1 2 3
}
```

Marking vertices as visited when you enqueue them (not when you dequeue) stops them from being added twice.

Depth-first search explores one branch as far as it goes before backtracking. With an adjacency list it takes O(V + E) time.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

void dfs(const std::vector<std::vector<int>>& adj, int u, std::vector<bool>& visited) {
    visited[u] = true;
    std::cout << u << " ";
    for (int v : adj[u]) {
        if (!visited[v]) dfs(adj, v, visited);
    }
}

int main() {
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> adj = {{1, 2}, {3}, {3}, {}};
    std::vector<bool> visited(adj.size(), false);
    dfs(adj, 0, visited);   // 0 1 3 2
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

Recursive DFS can overflow the call stack on very deep graphs; an explicit `std::stack` is the alternative.

In C++20, `std::erase(v, value)` removes all matching elements. Before C++20, use the erase-remove idiom, because `std::remove` only rearranges elements and does not change the vector's size.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 2, 4};

    std::erase(v, 2);   // C++20: removes every 2

    // Before C++20:
    // v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 2), v.end());   // needs <algorithm>

    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";   // 1 3 4
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

Use `std::unique` to move consecutive duplicates to the end, then erase the tail.

```cpp
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4};   // must be sorted
    auto last = std::unique(v.begin(), v.end());
    v.erase(last, v.end());

    for (int x : v) std::cout << x << " ";   // 1 2 3 4
    std::cout << "\n";
}
```

On an already sorted vector this is O(n). If the vector isn't sorted, `std::sort` it first, which costs O(n log n).

Use a `std::unordered_map` and increment the count for each element.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> nums = {1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3};
    std::unordered_map<int, int> freq;
    for (int x : nums) ++freq[x];

    for (const auto& [value, count] : freq)   // iteration order is unspecified
        std::cout << value << " appears " << count << " times\n";
}
```

Expected time is O(n) and space is O(k) for k distinct values. Use `std::map` instead if you need the keys sorted.

Compare characters from both ends with two pointers and stop when they meet; return false on the first mismatch.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <string>

bool isPalindrome(const std::string& s) {
    if (s.empty()) return true;
    size_t i = 0, j = s.size() - 1;
    while (i < j) {
        if (s[i] != s[j]) return false;
        ++i;
        --j;
    }
    return true;
}

int main() {
    std::cout << std::boolalpha << isPalindrome("racecar") << "\n";   // true
}
```

This takes O(n) time and O(1) extra space. If the problem ignores case, spaces, or punctuation, skip or normalize those characters first.

Put the read itself in the loop condition.

```cpp
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int value, sum = 0;
    while (std::cin >> value) {   // stops on EOF or invalid input
        sum += value;
    }
    std::cout << sum << "\n";
}
```

Avoid `while (!std::cin.eof())`: EOF is only set after a read attempt fails, so the loop runs one time too many. To read whole lines, use `while (std::getline(std::cin, line))`.
